#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:6
msgid "Extending Foreign Types"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:8
msgid ""
"An **extension trait** is a local trait definition whose primary purpose is "
"to attach new methods to foreign types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:12
msgid "// Copyright 2025 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:13
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:28
msgid "// Bring the extension trait into scope...\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:30
msgid "// ...then invoke its methods as if they were inherent methods\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:31
msgid "\"dad\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:32
msgid "\"grandma\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:38
msgid ""
"The `Ext` suffix is conventionally attached to the name of extension traits."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:40
msgid ""
"It communicates that the trait is primarily used for extension purposes, and "
"it is therefore not intended to be implemented outside the crate that "
"defines it."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:44
msgid ""
"Refer to the [\"Extension Trait\" RFC](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/0445-"
"extension-trait-conventions.html) as the authoritative source for naming "
"conventions."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:47
msgid ""
"The extension trait implementation for a foreign type must be in the same "
"crate as the trait itself, otherwise you'll be blocked by Rust's [_orphan "
"rule_](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/blob/master/text/2451-re-"
"rebalancing-coherence.md#what-is-coherence-and-why-do-we-care)."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:51
msgid "The extension trait must be in scope when its methods are invoked."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:53
msgid ""
"Comment out the `use` statement in the example to show the compiler error "
"that's emitted if you try to invoke an extension method without having the "
"corresponding extension trait in scope."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:57
msgid ""
"The example above uses an [_underscore import_](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"stable/reference/items/use-declarations.html#r-items.use.as-underscore) "
"(`use ext::StringExt as _`) to minimize the likelihood of a naming conflict "
"with other imported traits."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:60
msgid ""
"With an underscore import, the trait is considered to be in scope and you're "
"allowed to invoke its methods on types that implement the trait. Its "
"_symbol_, instead, is not directly accessible. This prevents you, for "
"example, from using that trait in a `where` clause."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/leveraging-the-type-system/extension-traits/extending-foreign-types.md:65
msgid ""
"Since extension traits aren't meant to be used in `where` clauses, they are "
"conventionally imported via an underscore import."
msgstr ""
